= Green Tea (綠茶)

_Botanical scope: all four tea entries — green, oolong, red, pu-erh — share the same species,_ Camellia sinensis_, and differ_ by saṃskāra (processing) _alone. The substance-differentiation is by oxidation: green = unoxidized; oolong = partial (10–70%); red / Western "black" = full; pu-erh / 黑茶 = post-fermentation (microbial). This is the_ classical Chinese tea-pharmacy paradigm, _and its parallels with the ginger ārdraka / śuṇṭhī and the Chinese 生薑 / 乾薑 / 炮薑 sequences are deep: in all three cases, modern phytochemistry has vindicated the saṃskāra-by-saṃskāra differentiation molecule-for-molecule._

_Status (this entry — green tea): Chinese — fully classical via_ Lu Yu's Chajing _(陸羽《茶經》, c. 760 CE), the foundational Chinese-pharmacy / dietetics treatise on tea; entered the formal_ Bencao _tradition with Li Shizhen (1578), as tea_ postdates _the earliest_ Shennong Bencao Jing _layer. Ayurveda: not classical — tea entered India through 19th-c. British plantation cultivation; the modern_ chāy _tradition is post-classical and the placement below is reasoned. Greco-Roman / European: not classical — tea was unknown to the Mediterranean world; the Western tradition begins with 17th-c. Dutch and British East-India-Company trade._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Camellia sinensis_ (L.) Kuntze, family Theaceae. Two principal varieties: _C. sinensis_ var. _sinensis_ — the *small-leaf Chinese* type, dominant for green and most Chinese teas — and _C. sinensis_ var. _assamica_ (Mast.) Kitam. — the *large-leaf Assamese* type, dominant for Indian / Sri Lankan black teas and for pu-erh. The drug is the *young leaf* (varying by plucking standard: a single bud, or a bud with one or two leaves — the classical _yī yá yī yè_ 一芽一葉, _yī yá èr yè_ 一芽二葉, etc.). The genus _Camellia_ commemorates *Georg Joseph Kamel* (Latinized Camellus, 1661–1706), Czech-Moravian Jesuit missionary-botanist in the Philippines; the species epithet _sinensis_ = "Chinese."#footnote[Species identity and variety distinction: standard botanical authority (_Flora of China_, vol. 12); the var. _sinensis_ / var. _assamica_ distinction is the principal practical lever for the four tea categories' chemistry — verify against current taxonomic literature. Genus eponym: Linnaeus's 1735 _Systema Naturae_ and the standard botanical eponymic literature. Plucking-standard nomenclature (_yī yá yī yè_, etc.): tea-industry technical literature.]

*English:* _Tea_ — *one of the two great names of the substance* (the other being _chá_ / _chai_), and the famous example of two trade routes producing two name-families across the world's languages. The English _tea_ (and the Dutch _thee_, French _thé_, German _Tee_, Spanish _té_, Hebrew _teh_, etc.) comes through *Min Nan / Hokkien Chinese* (_tê_, the southern Chinese coastal pronunciation), *via Dutch maritime trade* with Fujian and Taiwan in the 17th c. — i.e. the *maritime* name. The other family — Mandarin _chá_ → Russian _чай / chay_, Persian / Hindi _chāy_, Turkish _çay_, Arabic _shāy_, Portuguese _chá_ (notably! Portuguese sided with the overland family via Macau), Greek _τσάι_ — comes through *Mandarin / Cantonese*, *via overland Central-Asian trade* (and via the Portuguese maritime route, which uniquely sourced from Macau / Cantonese — explaining Portuguese's deviation from the rest of Western Europe). The world's *tea / chá* split mirrors *trade routes*, not language families. The Sinitic root is the same: 茶 (chá in Mandarin, _tê_ in Min Nan).#footnote[The "tea route" linguistic distribution is famously mapped in linguistic-geography literature; Dahl, "Linguistic atlas of pidgin and creole language structures" and standard etymological resources (OED, Houaiss for Portuguese _chá_) record the split. Standard linguistic explanation: Min Nan _tê_ via Dutch maritime trade vs. Mandarin _chá_ via overland Central-Asian / Portuguese-Macanese trade. _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "tea, n.¹"); Houaiss, _Dicionário Houaiss_, on _chá_; Russian etymological dictionaries on _чай_.]

*Chinese:* 茶 (chá) — the canonical character; standardized by *Lu Yu* (陸羽) in his foundational *Chajing* (茶經, "Classic of Tea," c. 760 CE) as a single-radical reduction from the older 荼 (tú, which had referred to several bitter plants including tea). The shorter 茶 with grass / vegetation radical (草 simplified as 艹 above) and the phonetic 余 below is Lu Yu's reform, and it has been the canonical character since. 綠茶 (lǜchá, "green tea") is the modern compound; the classical literature usually says simply 茶 (chá) for what we now call green tea, the unoxidized form being the dominant historical type. Specific Chinese terms relevant to green-tea drug-form distinctions:
- *茶葉 (cháyè)* — the leaf;
- *茶末 (chámò)* — powdered tea (the historical *Tang and Song* preparation, before brewing supplanted whisking);
- *茶湯 / 茶水 (chátāng / cháshuǐ)* — the infusion;
- *殺青 (shāqīng, "kill green")* — the foundational tea-processing step: high-heat denaturation of polyphenol oxidase (by pan-frying for most Chinese green teas, or steaming for Japanese green teas), arresting oxidation. The presence or absence of 殺青 *defines* whether the tea is "green" (殺青-arrested oxidation) or one of the oxidized categories.#footnote[Character standardization 荼 → 茶 by Lu Yu: _Chajing_ (茶經), c. 760 CE; standard discussion in Benn, _Tea in China: A Religious and Cultural History_ (Univ. of Hawai'i Press, 2015) and in Chinese tea-history literature. The 殺青 (kill green) processing step is the foundational distinction between green tea and all other oxidized categories; this is one of the cleanest cases of *processing-creates-substance* in food / drug technology.]

*Sanskrit:* No classical entry. Modern Hindi / Sanskrit: चाय (_cāy_, _chāy_, _cāyaḥ_) — from Persian via the same overland chā-family etymology. Tea is post-classical to South Asia (introduced by British plantation cultivation in Assam and Darjeeling, mid-19th c.). The modern *chai* tradition with milk and spices is a 19th–20th-c. innovation.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

Tea entered the formal Chinese medical literature *after* the earliest _Shennong Bencao Jing_ layer but *before* the major Tang-period synthesis. It is treated extensively in the *Lu Yu Chajing* (760 CE — the foundational text) and is given a full Bencao entry by *Li Shizhen* (_Bencao Gangmu_, 1578) in the grain / drink section. The classical Chinese reading is that tea is *cool*, *bitter-sweet*, and primarily a Lung-and-Liver-clearing daily drink of the literate and cultivated classes.#footnote[Lu Yu, _Chajing_ (茶經), c. 760 CE — the foundational Chinese tea text, with both technical and medical-dietetic content; English translations include Carpenter (1974) and Reichelt. Tea entry in _Bencao Gangmu_, 1578; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004) for the modern formal-medicine presentation.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cool (涼) to cold (寒) — green tea is the *coolest* of the four tea forms, the foundational "cooling" classification. (Compare: oolong is neutral-to-cool; red tea is warming; pu-erh is warming, especially ripe.)

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Bitter (苦), sweet (甘) — the canonical bitter-sweet pairing, with the bitter dominant in light infusion and the sweet emerging on aftertaste (_huígān_ 回甘, the "returning sweetness").

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Heart (心), Lung (肺), Stomach (胃), Liver (肝), Kidney (腎). The channel scope is broad — green tea's dietetic-medicinal reach covers the upper body (Lung-Heart, for clearing heat-related restlessness and dispersing summer-heat), the middle (Stomach, for clearing damp-heat), and the upper-orifice / sensory functions (Liver and brightening the eyes 明目).

*Actions & indications:*
- 清熱 — clears heat (the primary action).
- 消暑 — dispels summer-heat (the canonical summer drink in heat-affected climates).
- 解毒 — resolves toxicity.
- 化痰 — transforms phlegm (especially yellow / sticky / heat-pattern phlegm).
- 利水 — promotes urination, drains water (the mild diuretic).
- 明目 — brightens the eyes (classical scholar-class use, for screen-related and heat-related eye irritation in the modern restatement).
- 提神 / 醒腦 — refreshes the spirit and awakens the mind (the caffeine-and-theanine cognitive support).
- 消食 — disperses food stagnation (the after-meal digestive use).
- *Modern Chinese dietetic restatement:* aids weight-related metabolic regulation, supports cardiovascular health — these are modern functional-medicine framings of the classical 利水 + 化痰 + 消食 axes.#footnote[Action list: _Chajing_ (chapters 1, 6, 7); _Bencao Gangmu_ 茶 entry; modern systematization in Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; tea-dietetics literature (e.g. Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_). The 回甘 (returning sweetness) sensory category is canonical tea-tasting vocabulary.]

*Cautions:* Cool nature — unsuitable in Spleen-Stomach vacuity-cold (chronic loose stools, cold-pattern dyspepsia), Vāta-equivalent dryness, and depletion states. *Classical warning against heavy late-evening green tea* (disturbs sleep, depletes _shén_); *against drinking on empty stomach* (excess astringency on the gastric mucosa — the "tea drunkenness" 茶醉, with hypoglycemic-and-stomach-irritation symptoms — well documented in Chinese tea-culture); *against drinking with iron supplements* (the tannin-iron binding reduces absorption; relevant in iron-deficiency anaemia); *against drinking with medications taken with full glass of water* (the polyphenol fraction may bind some drugs).#footnote[Tea-drunkenness 茶醉: standard Chinese tea-culture knowledge; documented in tea-dietetics literature and as a folk-medical observation. Iron-tannin binding: well-documented in modern nutrition science.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry. The placement below is reasoned cross-system extension, anchored on the Chinese / Bencao / Chajing tradition and the modern phytochemistry. Not citation._

*Reasoned rasa:* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — dominant from the catechin / polyphenol content), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — secondary, from the tannin fraction).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp — the stimulant character from caffeine).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — converges with the Chinese reading. Green tea is the cooling tea.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha (the laghu-rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa profile); aggravates Vāta (the laghu-rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa with caffeine's drying-stimulant action); broadly Pitta-neutral or mildly pacifying (the cooling vīrya offsetting the tīkṣṇa-tikṣṇa pungent vipāka). *Vāta-aggravating in excess* is the principal reasoned dietary caution.

*Reasoned karma:* _Medhya_ (intellect-supporting — modern caffeine/L-theanine pharmacology), _kaphahara_ (Kapha-clearing), _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_ (breath-clearing), _mūtrala_ (mild diuretic), _dīpana / pācana_ in modest amounts (mild digestive), _hṛd-rakṣaka_ (cardiotonic — modern), _laghu-bṛṃhaṇa_ in an L-theanine-induced calm-alert state; in excess, _nidrā-nāśana_ (sleep-destroying), _rasa-kṣaya_ (fluid-depleting), _vāta-prakopa_ (Vāta-disturbing). All reasoned, not classical Sanskrit citation.#footnote[The reasoned dravyaguna placement closely parallels how P. V. Sharma (_Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II) places coffee (_kāphī_) — both are caffeine-bearing stimulant aromatics in a Kapha-clearing, Vāta-Pitta-disturbing register; green tea differs in the cooler vīrya and the lower caffeine load. Explicitly flagged as reasoned, not classical.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

No classical Ayurvedic viruddha. The Chinese and modern cautions apply.

- *(F) Chinese folk:* don't drink green tea on empty stomach; don't drink late-evening; don't drink with iron supplements; don't drink with hot-spicy meals (the tannin-irritation combination); don't drink with milk (a folk preference, not a viruddha, but milk-bound tannins reduce some of the catechin bioavailability).
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Caffeine load:* ~25–50 mg caffeine per cup of brewed green tea (less than coffee's ~80–100 mg/cup); standard caffeine cautions apply for pregnancy, arrhythmia, anxiety disorders, insomnia.
  - *Iron absorption:* the catechins (especially EGCG) bind non-heme iron and reduce its absorption substantially (~50–60% reduction when consumed with the meal); important in iron-deficiency anaemia, pregnancy, vegetarian diets. *Separate iron-rich meals from tea consumption by 1–2 hours.*
  - *Drug interactions:* catechins inhibit CYP3A4 and other drug-metabolizing enzymes (less so than grapefruit juice but real); EGCG specifically interferes with proteasome inhibitors (bortezomib) used in multiple myeloma — *a documented oncology-relevant interaction*; also interferes with the activity of some chemotherapy agents; mild antiplatelet effect.
  - *Liver:* *high-dose EGCG supplements* (concentrated capsules; not brewed tea) have been associated with *rare but real hepatotoxicity*; the EFSA opinion sets an upper-limit guidance for EGCG from supplements. The brewed-tea form has not been linked to this; the supplement-form concern is real.
  - *Oxalates:* moderate oxalate content; relevant in calcium-oxalate kidney-stone-formers (limit excess).
  - *Fluoride:* tea leaves accumulate fluoride from soil; very heavy long-term consumption of low-grade tea has been documented to cause dental and skeletal fluorosis in some Tibetan / Central-Asian populations relying on brick tea as a major daily intake.#footnote[Iron-tannin interaction: well-documented in nutrition literature. EGCG supplement hepatotoxicity: EFSA opinion (2018) on EGCG from supplements; pharmacovigilance literature. EGCG / bortezomib interaction: oncology pharmacology literature (Nahleh, _Cancer Chemotherapy and Pharmacology_; verify against current systematic reviews). Skeletal fluorosis from brick tea: documented in Tibetan-region public-health literature.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Tea is_ marginal in the medieval Arabic Tibb canon. _Tea entered the Persian-Arabic-Ottoman world only in the 17th–19th c., via Russian-Central-Asian and direct Chinese trade routes — postdating Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār, al-Rāzī, and the entire classical Tibb corpus by 600+ years. The modern Arabic-Persian-Urdu_ shāy / chā'y / chāy (شاي / چای / چائے) _is direct from Chinese 茶 (Mandarin chá) via overland Central-Asian transmission — the same chā-family etymology that gives Russian чай, Persian chā'y, Urdu chāy. Post-classical Tibb-e Sonnati (Persian medicine) treats tea as Cold and Dry in the 1st degree; muqawwī al-dimāgh (brain tonic via mild caffeine); muqawwī al-mi'da; muḥaffif al-ṣafrā' (refines bile); muḥsin al-hawḍm (digestive). Cautions: heavy long-term use yābis al-mizāj (drying); empty-stomach use as in Chinese folk; iron-binding. The modern Arab and Persian tea-drinking culture (Egyptian shāy with mint, Iranian chā'y in samovar, Maghrebi Tuareg "Sahara tea" with mint and sugar) is the substantive cultural register; the formal Tibb pharmacology is post-classical. No Avicennan or pre-modern attestation. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _for the broader framework._

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

The classical Chinese formal-pharmacy literature treats tea less as a compound-formula ingredient than as a *daily dietetic medicinal* and an *ad-hoc preparation*; the formal tea-pharmacy is in Lu Yu's _Chajing_ rather than in the Shanghan-Lun formulary tradition.

- *清茶 (qīngchá)* — the plain green-tea infusion; the canonical daily preparation.
- *龍井 (Longjing, Dragon Well)* — Hangzhou (West Lake region) flat-pan-fried green tea; among the most famous Chinese green teas; classical "imperial" prestige.
- *碧螺春 (Biluochun, Snail-Shell Spring)* — Jiangsu (Lake Taihu); spiraled small-leaf green tea; classical prestige tea.
- *黃山毛峰 (Huangshan Maofeng)* — Anhui; small-leaf, downy-tipped green tea.
- *信陽毛尖 (Xinyang Maojian)* — Henan; the northern Chinese green-tea reference.
- *太平猴魁 (Taiping Houkui)* — Anhui; long-leaf high-mountain green tea.
- *安吉白茶 (Anji Baicha)* — Zhejiang; despite the name "white tea," this is a *green* tea (low-chlorophyll cultivar processed in the green-tea way), highly amino-acid-rich.
- *六安瓜片 (Liuan Guapian)* — Anhui; leaf-only (no buds), pan-roasted.
- *Topical and compounded uses:* tea-based eye washes; tea + salt mouthwash for sore throat; tea decoctions for mild diarrhea (the astringent / tannin action); tea-leaf paste for minor burns.
- *Historic preparations:* Tang-era *tea cakes* (compressed bricks of crushed leaves, boiled in salted water with spices — the preparation Lu Yu's _Chajing_ describes; closer in form to modern pu-erh than to modern brewed loose tea); Song-era *whisked powdered tea* (the form preserved unchanged in Japanese matcha tradition).#footnote[Lu Yu, _Chajing_, on Tang-era preparation; Benn, _Tea in China_ (Hawai'i, 2015) on the historical preparation transitions. The flat-pan-fired green-tea processing technique is documented in Chinese tea-industry literature. Specific famous green teas: Benn, op. cit.; Chinese tea-culture literature.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas use Camellia sinensis. The modern Indian chai tradition — green tea or black tea brewed with milk, ginger, cardamom, cinnamon, black pepper, clove — is a 19th–20th-c. innovation that has acquired enormous cultural standing without classical pharmacological status. It is functional in a dietetic register (the milk + ginger + cardamom moderates the tea's Vāta-aggravating and Pitta-aggravating tendencies) and is consistent with reasoned Ayurvedic principles, but it is not a saṃhitā formula.#footnote[The Indian chai / masala chai tradition: 19th–20th-c. development; well documented in South Asian food-history literature. The reasoned Ayurvedic moderation of caffeine-bearing brews with milk-spice accompaniments is a continuous folk practice.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

Tea was unknown to the classical Greco-Roman world (Camellia sinensis is exclusively South / East Asian).

- *17th-c. European introduction:* Dutch East India Company (VOC) brought tea to Europe from the 1610s onward (the first documented shipment ~1610, from Hirado, Japan); Portuguese trade had brought small quantities via Macau slightly earlier. The Dutch introduced tea to the Netherlands, England (via Catherine of Braganza, the Portuguese-born wife of Charles II, who popularized tea-drinking at the English court in the 1660s), France (less successfully), and the broader European market.
- *18th c.:* tea becomes the British national drink; the British East India Company industrialized tea trade; the *Boston Tea Party* (1773) was the famous American colonial protest against British tea taxation.
- *19th c.:* the British develop tea cultivation in Assam (1820s onward) — the discovery of _C. sinensis_ var. _assamica_ as a native wild plant in Assam — and in Darjeeling (1850s); Sri Lanka (Ceylon) develops tea cultivation after the coffee blight (1860s). The British Empire is built partly on tea; the *tea trade with China (paying in silver, then opium) is the proximate cause of the Opium Wars*.
- *European pharmacy:* tea entered the European pharmacopoeial literature in the 18th c. as a stimulant and diuretic; rarely as a major drug. The major Western tradition is *culinary-cultural* (afternoon tea, high tea, milk-and-sugar British tradition) rather than pharmaceutical.
- *Modern Western:* the recent (post-1990) "green tea as health drink" Western adoption — *catechins, EGCG, anti-cancer claims* — is the modern phytomedical / nutraceutical framing, dominated by Western marketing and Japanese-influenced consumer culture; clinically the evidence is *more modest than the marketing suggests*.#footnote[Tea in early-modern Europe: Pettigrew, _A Social History of Tea_ (Stationery Office, 2001); Mintz, _Sweetness and Power_ (Viking, 1985) on the British Empire's sugar-and-tea economy. Tea-and-opium-wars: standard imperial history. Modern Western "superfood" framing of green tea / EGCG: marketing-dominated; clinical evidence modest — per the project's "don't overstate" rule.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (green tea — the unoxidized form):*
- *Catechins (~25–35% of dry leaf):* *epigallocatechin gallate (EGCG)* — the dominant member, ~50–80% of total catechins, the principal modern target compound; *epicatechin (EC)*, *epigallocatechin (EGC)*, *epicatechin gallate (ECG)*. These flavan-3-ols are the foundational green-tea molecules; *they decline sharply with oxidation* (and are converted to theaflavins and thearubigins in red / black teas, and to mixed oxidation products in oolong).
- *Caffeine (~2–4% of dry leaf):* the principal CNS stimulant, present across all four tea categories.
- *L-theanine (~1–2% of dry leaf):* a unique amino-acid (5-N-ethyl-L-glutamine) of tea, contributing the umami flavor and (modern pharmacology) producing the "calm alert" state in combination with caffeine — *a real and well-characterized pharmacological feature of tea that distinguishes it from coffee*.
- *Other amino acids:* glutamic acid, asparagine, arginine.
- *Polysaccharides:* substantial; some immunomodulatory.
- *Carotenoids and chlorophylls:* the green color of unoxidized leaf and the photo-protective action.
- *Saponins:* trace.
- *Fluoride:* the leaf accumulates fluoride from soil; up to ~1 mg / cup in some samples — relevant for very heavy long-term consumption.
- *Trace metals:* aluminium accumulation noted in some surveys; the bioavailable fraction is small.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); reviews in _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_, _Phytochemistry_, _Food Chemistry_; current systematic reviews. The catechin-to-theaflavin oxidation pathway is the foundational chemistry of the four tea categories; well-characterized.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antioxidant:* substantial in vitro; in vivo translation is modest and dose-dependent (and the ORAC-style claims are overblown — see açaí entry for the in-vitro / in-vivo translation problem).
- *Cardiovascular:* moderate evidence for modest improvements in lipid profile, blood-pressure, endothelial function with regular tea consumption (meta-analyses cluster around small effects); the East-Asian-population observational data is stronger than Western RCT data.
- *Caffeine pharmacology:* applies fully; lower caffeine per cup than coffee makes tea a gentler stimulant.
- *L-theanine and caffeine synergy:* well-characterized in cognitive-pharmacology literature — L-theanine *attenuates* caffeine's jitter-and-arousal and *enhances* its alertness / focus effects; the "calm alert" subjective state.
- *EGCG cancer-research literature:* substantial in vitro and animal-model activity; *clinical translation has been disappointing* for major cancers; *EGCG is interesting research compound, not a clinical anti-cancer drug*. The popular "green tea prevents cancer" claim is not supported at the strong-effect level by current evidence; modest reduction in *some* East-Asian-population cancer incidence in heavy green-tea drinkers (observational); causal evidence weak.
- *Weight-loss claims:* the catechin-and-caffeine combination produces *modest, transient* thermogenesis; clinical-trial weight-loss effects are *small* (typically 1–2 kg over months); popular claims substantially exceed evidence.
- *Antibacterial / antiviral:* EGCG has broad in vitro activity; clinical relevance modest.
- *Iron-binding:* substantial; the principal clinically-relevant green-tea side-effect for iron-deficiency.
- *Diabetic / glycemic:* modest evidence for improved insulin sensitivity with regular tea consumption.
- *Hepatotoxicity (supplement form):* see Viruddha (M).#footnote[Clinical evidence: Cochrane reviews, _BMJ_, _American Journal of Clinical Nutrition_, _Phytomedicine_, _Journal of Nutrition_; verify against current systematic reviews. The popular EGCG-anticancer claim is at substantial variance with rigorous clinical evidence — flagged per project rule. L-theanine pharmacology: cognitive-pharmacology and EEG literature.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Brewed green tea:* 3–5 cups/day (~600–1000 mL) of brewed tea is the typical "moderate" consumption range; well-tolerated in non-iron-deficient adults; caffeine load (~100–250 mg) is well within standard caffeine guidance.
- *EGCG supplements:* EFSA upper-limit guidance is *~800 mg EGCG/day* from supplements; *brewed-tea EGCG is unlikely to reach this even at heavy consumption*, but capsule users should be aware. The hepatotoxicity case literature is concentrated at >800 mg/day in supplements.
- *Pregnancy:* limit total caffeine to ≤200 mg/day (current obstetric guidance); 2–3 cups brewed green tea/day is appropriate; high-dose EGCG supplements not recommended.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid):* (a) *iron-deficiency anaemia* — separate tea from iron-rich meals by 1–2 hours; (b) *pregnancy* — limit total caffeine; (c) *known hepatotoxic reaction* to EGCG supplements; (d) concurrent *bortezomib* (multiple myeloma chemotherapy) — *avoid*; (e) concurrent *anticoagulants* (mild interaction); (f) *severe insomnia / anxiety disorders*; (g) *very heavy long-term consumption of low-grade brick tea* (fluorosis); (h) *infants / young children* — minimize.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Caffeine and EGCG limits: EFSA Scientific Opinions on caffeine and EGCG; FDA / ACOG / NICE pregnancy guidance — verify current versions. Risk-group flags are clinically-relevant cautions; reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice.]

